{"entryType":"UniProtKB reviewed (Swiss-Prot)","primaryAccession":"P0A206","secondaryAccessions":["P41794"],"uniProtkbId":"EUTJ_SALTY","entryAudit":{"firstPublicDate":"2005-03-01","lastAnnotationUpdateDate":"2025-06-18","lastSequenceUpdateDate":"2005-03-01","entryVersion":85,"sequenceVersion":1},"annotationScore":3.0,"organism":{"scientificName":"Salmonella typhimurium (strain LT2 / SGSC1412 / ATCC 700720)","taxonId":99287,"lineage":["Bacteria","Pseudomonadati","Pseudomonadota","Gammaproteobacteria","Enterobacterales","Enterobacteriaceae","Salmonella"]},"proteinExistence":"2: Evidence at transcript level","proteinDescription":{"recommendedName":{"fullName":{"value":"Ethanolamine utilization protein EutJ"}}},"genes":[{"geneName":{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000303","source":"PubMed","id":"7868611"}],"value":"eutJ"},"orderedLocusNames":[{"value":"STM2462"}]}],"comments":[{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"27063436"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"10464203"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305","source":"PubMed","id":"7868611"}],"value":"May protect ethanolamine ammonia-lyase (EAL, eutB-eutC) from inhibition, may function in assembling the bacterial microcompartment and/or in refolding EAL, suggesting it may have chaperone activity (Probable). Overexpression of eutJ and eutS in E.coli leads to multiple BMC-like structures; eutS expression alone leads to 1 BMC-like structure per cell (PubMed:27063436)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"29531136"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Expression of the eut operon allows this bacteria to use ethanolamine (EA) as a carbon, nitrogen and energy source. It relies on cobalamin (vitamin B12) both as a cofactor for the ethanolamine ammonia-lyase (EAL) activity and to induce the operon (PubMed:3045078). EA enhances bacterial survival in macrophages in a concentration-dependent manner, suggesting it is an important nutrient during infection (PubMed:29531136)"}],"commentType":"FUNCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Amine and polyamine degradation; ethanolamine degradation"}],"commentType":"PATHWAY"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"3045078"}],"value":"Part of the 17-gene eut operon transcribed from a single promoter, induced by ethanolamine and adenosylcobalamin (AdoCbl, vitamin B12)"}],"commentType":"INDUCTION"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"10464203"},{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000269","source":"PubMed","id":"16585748"}],"value":"Not required for aerobic growth on ethanolamine (EA) supplemented with cobalamin (vitamin B12). A double eutJ-eutG deletion is not required for growth in the above conditions. A double eutG-eutH deletion is not required for growth in the above conditions. Slightly attenuated in a mouse model of infection (PubMed:10464203). A non-polar deletion mutant grows on EA from pH 5.5 to pH 8.0, but does not grow at pH 8.5, no change in acetaldehyde release on EA plus vitamin B12 (PubMed:16585748)"}],"commentType":"DISRUPTION PHENOTYPE"},{"texts":[{"evidences":[{"evidenceCode":"ECO:0000305"}],"value":"Belongs to the EutJ family"}],"commentType":"SIMILARITY"}],"features":[{"type":"Chain","location":{"start":{"value":1,"modifier":"EXACT"},"end":{"value":279,"modifier":"EXACT"}},"description":"Ethanolamine utilization protein EutJ","featureId":"PRO_0000087090"}],"keywords":[{"id":"KW-0143","category":"Molecular function","name":"Chaperone"},{"id":"KW-1185","category":"Technical term","name":"Reference proteome"}],"references":[{"referenceNumber":1,"citation":{"id":"7868611","citationType":"journal article","authors":["Stojiljkovic I.","Baeumler A.J.","Heffron F."],"citationCrossReferences":[{"database":"PubMed","id":"7868611"},{"database":"DOI","id":"10